There's other times when the network says to you, we've got the Victoria's Secret models, and you're going to use them because we're doing a prime time Victoria's Secret Fashion Show special here on CBS, and you have to use them on your show, and we'll promote your show more because it's a crossover with our big Victoria's Secret Fashion a word, whatever in God's name it was, and we'll advertise the hell out of you, we'll give you this many more promos and we're in season, right, we're in early season, early mid season three here, we are again to remind everybody, not a guaranteed hit show, we are not an all time, like on the list of sitcoms that went a long time and had a lot of fans, we are not that show yet, we are frightened youngsters in our early thirties being told, You're using these victorious secret models.
但也有其他时候，电视网会对你说：“我们有维多利亚的秘密模特，你们得用她们，因为我们要在这里——CBS电视台，举办黄金时段的维多利亚的秘密时尚秀特别节目，你们必须在剧里安排她们出场，这样我们就会大力宣传你们的剧，因为这跟我们盛大的维多利亚的秘密时尚秀——不管它上帝保佑叫什么名字——进行了联动，我们会狠狠地帮你们做广告，给你们安排更多的宣传片，而且我们现在正值播出季，对吧，我们现在是第三季的初期，刚过季初，我们再次提醒大家，我们可不是那种稳操胜券的热门剧，我们还算不上那种经久不衰、拥有大批粉丝的情景喜剧，我们现在还不是那种剧，我们只是一群三十岁出头、吓得半死的年轻人，却被人告知：‘你们得用这些维多利亚的秘密模特。’”

00:09:47.279 --> 00:09:49.360
By the way, there's worst things to be told to do.
顺便说一句，还有比这更糟糕的要求呢。

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:54.120
I'm not saying as far as mafia threats go, it's not one of the scary little things.
我不是说从黑手党威胁的角度来看，这算不上什么可怕的小事。

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:55.360
I'm going to make you an offer you can't refuse.
就好像“我要开出一个你无法拒绝的条件”。

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:56.960
It's like, yeah, this is actually a pleasant offer.
感觉就像是，好吧，这其实是个挺让人愉快的条件。

00:09:56.960 --> 00:09:57.960
They're very attractive women.
她们是非常迷人的女性。

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:07.179
And I wrote you this little day to sing to you in New York City.
我还给你写了一首小曲儿在纽约市唱给你听。

00:10:07.179 --> 00:10:09.179
We'll be right back.
我们马上回来。

00:10:09.179 --> 00:10:15.049
Hi, I'm Alex Goldman, host of the Hyper-Fixed Podcast.
嗨，我是亚历克斯·戈德曼，《超级修复》播客的主持人。

00:10:15.049 --> 00:10:18.490
Each week, we take listeners' problems and try to solve them for them.
每周，我们都会收集听众的问题并试着帮他们解决。

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:21.690
Problems like, I'm 30 and I'm scared to drive in New York.
比如这些问题：我30岁了，但害怕在纽约开车。

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:25.450
Or, why can't I adjust the volume of my car stereo when I'm in reverse?
或者，为什么我挂倒档时无法调节汽车音响的音量？

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:27.970
We also solve non-car-related problems.
我们也会解决与汽车无关的问题。

00:10:27.970 --> 00:10:34.070
If you have a problem, not only will we fix it, we'll expose the hidden systems that cause that problem in the first place.
如果你有什么问题，我们不仅会帮你解决，还会揭示导致该问题的隐藏系统。

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:36.590
That's the Hyper-Fixed Podcast from Radiotopia.
这就是来自 Radiotopia 的《超级修复》播客。

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:51.490
Find it wherever you find podcasts or at HyperFixedPod.com.
你可以在各大播客平台或访问 HyperFixedPod.com 收听。

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:53.600
And now, back to the show.
现在，让我们回到节目中。

00:10:53.600 --> 00:11:00.750
A lot of great art doesn't start from, hey, we've got the Victoria's Secret models.
很多伟大的艺术作品并不是始于：“嘿，我们有维多利亚的秘密模特。”

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:08.574
I bet this will spark and enduring beautiful artistic message that you'll want to convey via the tools of these Victoria's Secret models.
我敢打赌，这会激发出一场持久而美丽的艺术讯息，而你想通过这些维多利亚的秘密模特作为工具来传达它。

00:11:08.574 --> 00:11:21.309
This was like, we've got this thing, we've strongly suggest if you want to have a lot of promotion and get good ratings that you use this thing we're offering you, and then we had to reverse engineer a whole episode around.
这感觉就像是：我们有这么个赞助安排，我们强烈建议，如果你想获得大量宣传并取得好收视率，最好用一下我们提供的这个安排，然后我们不得不围绕它反向构思出一整集的故事。

00:11:21.309 --> 00:11:23.389
You know, in some ways- How can you be creative with that?
你知道，从某种程度上来说——你怎么能用这种东西发挥创意呢？

00:11:23.389 --> 00:11:35.950
It was a five-minute version of he's the Cadillac of Rebound guys, which if you've been paying attention, this was- I blocked that one out.
这只是个五分钟版本的“他是备胎界的凯迪拉克”，如果你一直有关注的话，这就是——我把那个给屏蔽了。

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:37.669
It's season three, episode one.
那是第三季第一集。

00:11:37.669 --> 00:11:45.269
In an otherwise, I think, perfect scene between Ted and Robin talking about Gail, he confronts.
在一个我觉得本来很完美的泰德和罗宾讨论盖尔的场景中，他进行了对质。

00:11:45.269 --> 00:11:49.070
And there was this weird moment where Ted says, come on, he's the Cadillac of Rebound guys.
当时有一个很奇怪的时刻，泰德说：“拜托，他是备胎界的凯迪拉克。”

00:11:49.070 --> 00:11:56.350
And I reminded Craig that I'm pretty sure that Cadillac did like an extra super sponsorship of that evening's episode.
我提醒克雷格，我非常确信凯迪拉克对当晚的那集节目进行了某种超级赞助。

00:11:56.350 --> 00:12:02.029
And all we had to do was say the word Cadillac in a flattering way.
而我们要做的就是以一种奉承的方式说出“凯迪拉克”这个词。

00:12:02.029 --> 00:12:08.149
We had to say a Cadillac in a flattering way in a scene and one of the main characters like you had to say your Ted had to say it.
我们必须在一个场景中以奉承的方式提到凯迪拉克，而且其中一个主要角色得说出来，就像你得说，泰德得说。

00:12:08.149 --> 00:12:14.429
And we snuck that one line in and my brain deleted it like fucking eternal sunshine of the spot of this mind.
我们把那句台词偷偷塞了进去，结果我的大脑就像《美丽心灵的永恒阳光》里的情节一样，把它删得一干二净。

00:12:14.429 --> 00:12:18.830
And when you brought it up on the pot a few weeks ago, I was like blown the fuck away.
几周前当你在播客里提起这件事时，我整个人震惊得他妈的无以复加。

00:12:18.830 --> 00:12:20.509
I had completely forgotten it.
我完全把它忘光了。

00:12:20.509 --> 00:12:21.990
This one I haven't forgotten.
这个我没忘。

00:12:21.990 --> 00:12:22.830
The other one I haven't.
另一个我没忘。

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:23.669
I mean, there's a bunch of them.
我是说，这种事还有很多。

00:12:23.669 --> 00:12:24.590
I'll point this out as we go.
我们边走边指出吧。

00:12:24.590 --> 00:12:26.190
I think I forgot to point this out.
我觉得我刚才好像忘了指出这一点。

00:12:26.190 --> 00:12:30.389
At the end of season one, we're doing a podcast flashback here, whoosh, whip pan.
在第一季结束时，我们现在在做播客闪回，呼，镜头横摇。

00:12:30.389 --> 00:12:33.950
And we're back in the end of season one of the podcast.
然后我们回到了播客的第一季结尾。

00:12:33.950 --> 00:12:38.789
The finale of season one, which I love and is also, I think, an otherwise perfect episode.
第一季的季终集，我非常喜欢，而且我也觉得那是另一集完美无缺的剧集。

00:12:38.789 --> 00:12:42.870
I think, and I think we didn't fuck up the episode by doing this, I don't think.
我觉得，而且我觉得我们并没有因为这样做而搞砸那一集，我觉得没有。

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:46.870
We were told, you're gonna do a scene in Red Lobster.
当时有人对我们说：“你们要在红龙虾餐厅拍一场戏。”

00:12:46.870 --> 00:12:52.549
Which, again, was far less exciting than you're going to use the Victoria's Secret models.
这再次证明，这可比“你们要用维多利亚的秘密模特”要无趣得多。

00:12:52.549 --> 00:12:54.389
This was much more like, wait, Red Lobster?
这更像是：等等，红龙虾餐厅？

00:12:54.389 --> 00:13:04.470
And then we just came up with this thing that what if in the middle of Marshall and the least horrible gut-wrenching, heartbreaking breakup, they'd go, want to go to Red Lobster?
然后我们突然想出了这么个点子：如果马歇尔和莉莉正在经历最可怕、最揪心、最让人心碎的分手时，他们突然来了一句：“想去红龙虾餐厅吗？”

00:13:04.470 --> 00:13:15.669
And they go to Red Lobster, and we had this image where the only way it can be funny is if they're crying while eating Lobster, there was something that's so lobster so messy and then you're crying on top of eating Lobster.
他们去了红龙虾餐厅，我们当时设想唯一的笑点就是他们一边吃龙虾一边哭，龙虾吃起来那么狼吞虎咽、一团糟，然后你还在吃龙虾的时候哭出来。

00:13:15.669 --> 00:13:17.629
That was not a creative decision.
那可不是出于创作上的决定。

00:13:17.629 --> 00:13:19.710
It wasn't like, hey, you know what would be funny?
不是那种“嘿，你们知道什么会很有趣吗？”的想法。

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:23.870
Let's go, we had to use Red Lobster, just like we had to use the Victoria's Secret models.
让我们去吧，我们必须用红龙虾餐厅，就像我们必须用维多利亚的秘密模特一样。

00:13:23.870 --> 00:13:27.710
And I would say that one worked, cause we just made it funny and weird.
而且我要说那个点子管用了，因为我们把它搞得既搞笑又古怪。

00:13:27.710 --> 00:13:30.110
And this one was more like, Josh, you'll remember this.
但这个情节更像是——乔什，你会记得这个的。

00:13:30.110 --> 00:13:38.710
Like the other thing is the field trip effect where some of the energy of the show can be lost via that field trip or the gang is kind of off campus and it doesn't quite go great feeling.
还有一件事就是“郊游效应”，节目的部分活力可能会因为这种郊游而丧失，或者主角团算是离开校园了，感觉就有点不太对劲。

00:13:38.710 --> 00:13:48.470
The other thing that diffuses the energy where energy, there's an entropy that happens in the sort of energy of the show, is a gigantic fucking set.
另一个会消解活力的东西——节目活力中会产生一种熵增——就是一个巨大的该死的主景。

00:13:48.470 --> 00:13:49.990
That set was huge.
那个布景太大了。

00:13:49.990 --> 00:13:50.830
Do you remember that?
你还记得吗？

00:13:50.830 --> 00:13:51.669
The Victoria's Secret set?
维多利亚的秘密的布景？

00:13:51.669 --> 00:13:52.639
The huge set.
那个巨大的布景。

00:13:52.639 --> 00:13:53.309
Yes, yes.
对，对。

00:13:53.309 --> 00:14:00.070
And it was just all of the Victoria's Secret models are there, you have to move place to place in this giant set, 100 extras.
而且所有的维密模特都在那儿，你得在这个巨大的布景里换来换去，还有100个群演。

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:03.149
It's very hard to make those scenes good, I find.
我发现要把那些场景拍好真的很难。

00:14:03.149 --> 00:14:06.309
I remember it being like tense.
我记得当时感觉气氛很紧张。

00:14:06.309 --> 00:14:09.950
Like you didn't feel, I felt like I was a guest star on some other show.
就好像你没有那种感觉……我觉得自己像是其他某个剧里的客串明星。

00:14:09.950 --> 00:14:10.789
Yes, really?
是吗，真的吗？

00:14:10.789 --> 00:14:13.110
I didn't feel like I was on my show.
我感觉不到我是在拍自己的剧。

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:18.389
I felt like Neil and I were guest starring in another alternate universe.
我觉得我和尼尔就像是在另一个平行宇宙里客串。

00:14:18.389 --> 00:14:19.830
You were doing a cameo.
你们就是在客串。

00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:32.230
I do want to say though, when you get these mafia requests from the network, they can go one of two ways and I think the Red Lobster solution was as elegant a solution as you could have.
不过我确实想说，当你收到电视台这种“黑手党式”的要求时，通常会有两种发展方向，我觉得红龙虾的解决方案已经算得上是你能想到的最优雅的解法了。

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:43.701
But I remember when I was in college, I had a playwriting teacher who said, sometimes like saying you have to write three scenes and one object, the same object is dropped in both scenes.
但我记得我上大学时，我有一个编剧老师说过，有时就像要求你写三个场景和一个物件，同一个物件在两个场景中都要出现。

00:14:43.701 --> 00:14:45.787
That was the kind of order, right?
就是那种命题，对吧？

00:14:45.787 --> 00:14:47.259
Mine was a severed head.
我当时的是一个断头。

00:14:47.259 --> 00:14:51.983
I don't want to get it more into it, but it was this very dark scene I wrote.
我不想深入多说了，但我写的是一个非常黑暗的场景。

00:14:51.983 --> 00:14:58.057
And I think that sometimes a weird request like that can actually get you can jog something in you.
我觉得有时候像那样奇怪的要求其实能激发你的灵感。

00:14:58.057 --> 00:14:59.468
I think mostly it does.
我觉得大部分时候确实如此。

00:14:59.468 --> 00:15:08.486
I think restriction can be like necessity as the mother of invention, like being painted in a corner can be wonderfully creative, except this time.
我认为限制就像是“需求是发明之母”一样，被逼入绝境有时能迸发出奇妙的创造力，除了这一次。

00:15:08.486 --> 00:15:13.332
Well, if they said like, Victoria's Secret models, but you can set it anywhere.
好吧，如果他们说的是，比如维密模特，但你可以把场景设在任何地方。

00:15:13.332 --> 00:15:17.319
But the thing was Victoria's Secret after, you know, after party.
但关键是维密派对之后，你知道的，会后派对。

00:15:17.319 --> 00:15:22.841
The thing I liked about it is Barney getting the yips at the worst possible moment, right?
我喜欢的部分是巴尼在最糟糕的时刻怯场了，对吧？

00:15:22.841 --> 00:15:24.681
This is Barney's World Series.
对巴尼来说这就是他的世界大赛。

00:15:24.681 --> 00:15:26.215
This is getting the yips.
这就是怯场。

00:15:26.215 --> 00:16:05.600
This is like, You can't a pitcher can't throw a strike in the world right by the way My favorite version of the yips in real life was Mets fans of a certain age may remember there was a catcher named Mackie Sasser fucking great name, and he got the yips in the weirdest way I like to remember this he couldn't throw the ball back to the pitcher He got this thing where he would start to throw it and he frees and start to throw a freeze So it wasn't like it affected competition Maybe he had it a little bit trying to throw a guy out at second but it was like he had the yips about the simplest and an inconsequential transaction in the game.
这就好比一个投手在全世界面前投不出好球——顺便说一句，我在现实中最喜欢的怯场例子是，某个年龄段的大都会队球迷可能会记得，有个捕手叫麦基·萨瑟，一个真他妈棒的名字，他以最奇怪的方式怯场了。我记得是这样的：他无法把球回传给投手，他一准备回传就会卡住、僵住，开始传球然后僵住。所以这并不是说它影响了比赛，也许他在尝试把试图盗垒的人杀在二垒时有点这毛病，但这就好像他对比赛中最简单、最微不足道的动作感到了怯场。

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:08.759
Get the ball back to the pitcher so he can begin the next play.
把球传回给投手，好让他开始下一球。

00:16:08.759 --> 00:16:12.000
And he got the yips and it was just the strangest thing and it made this impression on me.
他就是怯场了，这太奇怪了，给我留下了深刻的印象。

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:14.080
It's the first time I heard the phrase the yips.
那是我第一次听到“怯场”（the yips）这个词。

00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:17.840
And I think the yips is better applied to like more important moments in sports.
而且我认为“怯场”这个词更适合用在体育运动中更关键的时刻。

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:20.320
And this is Barney's World Series, he gets the yips.
而这就是巴尼的世界大赛，他怯场了。

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:21.580
But that's the version of the yips.
但这就是那种怯场。

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:28.840
That's where I first heard that phrase of like, you just of everyday thing and you just get in your head and you can't do it.
那是第一次听到这个词，就是指你面对日常的事情，然后脑子里开始钻牛角尖，结果就做不成了。

00:16:28.840 --> 00:16:33.639
And it's also something you've done, something that's your either your profession or your great talent.
而且这也是你以前做过的事，是你的职业或者你引以为傲的天赋。

00:16:33.639 --> 00:16:37.120
Like it's the thing you've been trained to do mindlessly and reflexively.
就像那是你经过训练、能闭着眼睛凭本能去做的事情。

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:37.960
So I liked that.
所以我很喜欢这个设定。

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:38.779
That was our good idea.
那是我们的好点子。

00:16:38.779 --> 00:16:45.080
The good idea was what if that happens to him at the Victoria's Secret Party and the other good idea like in the episode, and again, Jamie Runheimer wrote a great script.
好点子就是：如果这种事发生他在维密派对上会怎么样？另一个好点子就像这一集里那样——再说一次，杰米·伦海默写了一个很棒的剧本。

00:16:45.080 --> 00:16:46.159
It's nothing to do.
这跟那个没关系。

00:16:46.159 --> 00:16:52.720
This is self critiquing as showrunners, storybreakers or whatever, this is a me problem.
这是作为剧集运作人、情节策划或者别的什么的自我批评，这是我个人的问题。

00:16:52.720 --> 00:17:07.539
But the concept of building yourself on this one little lie, like how much of Barney's personality was predicated on the idea that he, the first time he had sex rocked this woman's world, this older woman who had been with every guy.
但这个概念是建立在一个小谎言之上的，比如巴尼的个性有多少是建立在这样一个假设上：他第一次跟人发生性关系时，彻底震撼了一个经历过无数男人的成熟女性的世界。

00:17:07.539 --> 00:17:14.660
And that idea was funny that Barney so much, I like how it peeled back Barney's fragility emotionally.
而且巴尼的那个想法非常搞笑，我喜欢它剥离了巴尼在情感上的脆弱。

00:17:14.660 --> 00:17:23.140
And to see Barney also fail and fail and fail in this episode, I think that's a good medicine against the Barney seeming like the hero of the show.
看到巴尼在这个剧集里也不断地失败、失败、再失败，我认为这是对抗巴尼显得像是剧集英雄的一剂良药。

00:17:24.099 --> 00:17:25.819
And Wayne Brady is the other great thing in this episode.
韦恩·布雷迪是这集的另一个亮点。

00:17:25.819 --> 00:17:27.420
Oh yeah, Wayne's great in this episode.
哦，对，韦恩这集演得真好。

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:28.259
Yeah.
对。

00:17:28.259 --> 00:17:29.099
Hilarious in this episode.
这集里太搞笑惹。

00:17:29.099 --> 00:17:39.619
Yeah, there was something about the Victoria Secret scene that I agree with you that it was like his World Series, but it felt like curiously low stakes.
